A simple way to keep the original order is to make one list for positive values and another for negative values. After that, take one value from each list in turn and write it into the answer.

The two lists also make the code easy to check. The problem guarantees the same number of positive and negative values, so every pair can be filled safely. The original `nums` array is left unchanged.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[] rearrangeArray(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> positives = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> negatives = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value > 0) {
                positives.add(value);
            } else {
                negatives.add(value);
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[nums.length];
        int resultIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < positives.size(); i++) {
            result[resultIndex++] = positives.get(i);
            result[resultIndex++] = negatives.get(i);
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The running time is `O(n)`, and the extra space is `O(n)` because of the two lists and the returned array.

The result must start with a positive value, so positive numbers always belong at indexes `0, 2, 4...`. Negative numbers go to `1, 3, 5...`. Two write indexes are enough to place each value while scanning `nums` once.

For example, with `[3, 1, -2, -5, 2, -4]`, the placements are `3 -> 0`, `1 -> 2`, `-2 -> 1`, `-5 -> 3`, `2 -> 4`, and `-4 -> 5`. The order inside each sign group stays unchanged.

An empty input also returns an empty result. The input array is not changed.

